WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.832
- Guess who I'm with?

00:00:00.832 --> 00:00:02.800
The amazing
Gary Vaynerchuk!

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.633
CEO of VaynerMedia
among other things.

00:00:07.633 --> 00:00:09.000
Many other things.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.133
- Thank you.
- How's it going?

00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:12.400
- It's going really well,
it's great to be here.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.867
This is my 10th South-by
so it's changed quite a bit.

00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:18.700
When we used to
do things like this

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.433
it would be on
like a small FlipCam.

00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:24.533
Now there's very expensive
cameras and rigs and lighting.

00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:26.700
A lot of has changed,
a lot's the same,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:27.734
but I'm very happy to be here.

00:00:27.734 --> 00:00:29.900
Hello, everybody
that's in this room.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.533
Thrilled to spend
some time together.

00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:32.366
- Awesome.

00:00:32.366 --> 00:00:36.166
So you're always part of the
trends, you're forecasting.

00:00:36.166 --> 00:00:38.000
What's the thing we're
talking about this year?

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.867
What's exciting you?

00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:45.533
- Listen, the fact of
the matter is nine years ago,

00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:48.300
Twitter exploded here,
and then all of the sudden

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.166
South-by had to be this place
where things got launched.

00:00:51.166 --> 00:00:54.166
The reality is really, besides
Twitter and Foursquare,

00:00:54.166 --> 00:00:56.433
nothing's ever been
launched here, right?

00:00:56.433 --> 00:00:57.300
So--

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.600
- This is an opening
for you, why don't you

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.567
launch something here?

00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:02.800
- I think this notion
of like, what's exciting?

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.467
The same stuff
that's always been exciting,

00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:07.400
which is I'm not
super worried about features

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.600
or what's getting
covered the most by the media.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.867
I'm worried about
where consumer attention is

00:01:13.867 --> 00:01:17.200
and how do I storytell
natively to that platform

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.467
in that environment?

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:23.066
So things that I'm not
excited about are consumer VR.

00:01:23.066 --> 00:01:27.166
I do not think
consumer VR is here next week.

00:01:27.166 --> 00:01:29.266
I think we,
especially if you're at South-by

00:01:29.266 --> 00:01:30.834
in this lounge,
we're excited about it.

00:01:30.834 --> 00:01:33.500
We understand the
technology is there.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.500
It's exciting.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.900
But the day when an
American or a human being

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.734
spends three, four
hours a day in contact lenses

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:43.533
and lives in a virtual
world is much further away

00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:47.200
than the people that are in
that industry wish it to be.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.166
The things that excite me

00:01:48.166 --> 00:01:50.033
is that Facebook
ads are underpriced

00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:53.500
and Instagram and Snapchat
influencers are underpriced.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.367
And most people just
have opinions about it,

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:58.600
even very bright
people in this room

00:01:58.600 --> 00:01:59.967
and the people watching at home,

00:01:59.967 --> 00:02:02.333
and there are
very few practitioners

00:02:02.333 --> 00:02:04.934
in March 2017 marketing.

00:02:04.934 --> 00:02:07.166
And that's what excites
me because when you have lack

00:02:07.166 --> 00:02:10.433
of practitionership,
there's ability to exploit

00:02:10.433 --> 00:02:12.500
those opportunities
if you're deeply grounded

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:13.333
in those principles.

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:14.500
- [Shira] It's funny because
some people actually think

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.333
there was a bubble.

00:02:15.333 --> 00:02:16.967
I think there's always gonna be

00:02:16.967 --> 00:02:18.233
those case studies or
those circumstances--

00:02:18.233 --> 00:02:19.133
- What bubble?

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:20.967
- Well, of like the
influencer marketing bubble.

00:02:20.967 --> 00:02:23.100
- Influencer marketing
hasn't even started.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.900
Even though it's a one to three,

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.767
on people's best
guesses, billion dollar market,

00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:30.767
the reality is is
that influencer marketing

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:33.233
has always been here, right?

00:02:33.233 --> 00:02:35.600
John Wayne smoked Lucky Stripes.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:36.767
Somebody we're
paying attention to

00:02:36.767 --> 00:02:39.467
doing something with a
brand is tried and true.

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:42.233
It's just that Instagram
and Snapchat and Facebook

00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:45.800
and these platforms are at
such scale of distribution now

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.667
and there's no middleman.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:50.433
NBC and Comcast used
to get the middle price.

00:02:50.433 --> 00:02:52.500
Now the platform
is the distribution

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.166
and the end user's
getting those economics

00:02:54.166 --> 00:02:55.667
directly with the brand.

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.433
So we're living
through just the early stages

00:02:58.433 --> 00:03:01.967
of that genre, because
that's just tried and true.

00:03:01.967 --> 00:03:03.567
That just always has been here.

00:03:03.567 --> 00:03:05.133
It just looks a
little bit different

00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:06.800
than we saw it 40 years ago.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.367
- Now you're an
influencer, you could say,

00:03:08.367 --> 00:03:10.567
but you're also a CEO,
you're an entrepreneur,

00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:11.400
you're a businessman.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.867
How do you balance
between your own personal brand

00:03:13.867 --> 00:03:16.500
and then being
responsible to a company

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.367
and employees and all that?

00:03:18.367 --> 00:03:19.333
- [Gary] I run my company.

00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:22.700
I'm the CEO of an 800 person
$130 million a year business,

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.367
and that takes up all my time.

00:03:24.367 --> 00:03:26.834
The GaryVee thing
is my side hustle.

00:03:26.834 --> 00:03:30.200
I have a team that records
content in vlog form, we use--

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.934
- [Shira] He's right here, 
that guy.

00:03:31.934 --> 00:03:32.633
- DRock.

00:03:32.633 --> 00:03:33.800
- [Shira] Does he just travel with you?

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.567
- Yes, DRock is the
person I love the most.

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:37.400
(laughs)

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.633
DRock is with me all
the time and it's my hack,

00:03:39.633 --> 00:03:40.767
I figured it out.

00:03:40.767 --> 00:03:42.500
DRock films, we make a vlog.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:46.033
In that we have 18 hours worth
of content that's happening.

00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:48.266
A lot of those turn
into my Medium posts.

00:03:48.266 --> 00:03:50.400
A lot of those turn
into my meme videos.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.233
A lot of those turn
into my audio podcast.

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:57.734
I don't have time to
build a personal brand.

00:03:57.734 --> 00:03:58.600
I don't have time.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.266
I live my life
as an entrepreneur.

00:04:01.266 --> 00:04:05.166
And I don't win on
being attractive or funny,

00:04:05.166 --> 00:04:08.166
I win on being an
operator and being right

00:04:08.166 --> 00:04:09.734
about things that are happening,

00:04:09.734 --> 00:04:11.300
and that's just
my day-to-day life

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:12.834
and then we produce it at home.

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:15.633
But I spend all my
time running my company.

00:04:15.633 --> 00:04:17.367
When I do public speaking,

00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:19.800
that's business
development for VaynerMedia.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.467
It's a lot more fun
to get a $3 million scope

00:04:22.467 --> 00:04:24.834
doing a keynote at the ANA

00:04:24.834 --> 00:04:27.266
than being part
of an RFP process.

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:30.266
That's just smart business.

00:04:30.266 --> 00:04:32.567
I think being an influencer...

00:04:32.567 --> 00:04:33.967
First of all, 99% of people

00:04:33.967 --> 00:04:35.567
that say they're in an
influencer in their

00:04:35.567 --> 00:04:37.467
Instagram account
are full of shit,

00:04:37.467 --> 00:04:38.433
and for the 1%--

00:04:38.433 --> 00:04:40.367
- [Shira] Well don't put that
on your Instagram account.

00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:41.934
Like, I'm an influencer.

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:44.300
- Many do.
(Shira laughs)

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.700
And then I think
the ones that like,

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:49.166
being in influencer means that
you're bring value to people

00:04:49.166 --> 00:04:52.667
as a person living
and creating content

00:04:52.667 --> 00:04:55.200
through these platforms that
people are paying attention to.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.667
And so you've gotta
have a lot of talent

00:04:57.667 --> 00:04:58.834
to actually win that.

00:04:58.834 --> 00:05:01.467
For every Logan Paul,
who I see in the background,

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:05.166
there are 8,000,
80,000, 800,000 people

00:05:05.166 --> 00:05:08.266
that aspire to make
videos and pictures on Instagram

00:05:08.266 --> 00:05:09.633
and make a living out of it,

00:05:09.633 --> 00:05:11.700
and the reality is
they're not interesting enough

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.066
or smart enough
or talented enough.

00:05:13.066 --> 00:05:14.600
So it's just supply and demand

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.967
of are you able to
have somebody's attention.

00:05:16.967 --> 00:05:18.233
- And what is
someone supposed to do

00:05:18.233 --> 00:05:20.000
if they don't have
a person to run around

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:21.400
following them on a camera?

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.333
- [Gary] Do what I did for the
first 30 years of my life.

00:05:23.333 --> 00:05:25.233
Build a business that
allows you to afford one.

00:05:25.233 --> 00:05:26.333
(laughs)

00:05:26.333 --> 00:05:28.734
- That's good advice.

00:05:28.734 --> 00:05:30.867
- I think this, Shira, I think
a lot of people forget

00:05:30.867 --> 00:05:35.000
about my narrative because
of my spiel in this genre.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.467
I spent the first
15, 16 years of my life

00:05:38.467 --> 00:05:41.100
building an actual
wine retail business

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.600
before I even said
one word to the world

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.300
about my business thoughts.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.767
I didn't come out the
womb and say I'm an influencer.

00:05:47.767 --> 00:05:48.934
I built an actual business

00:05:48.934 --> 00:05:50.467
and then I talked
about building businesses.

00:05:50.467 --> 00:05:52.934
- [Shira] But the platforms
weren't there yet, right?

00:05:52.934 --> 00:05:53.900
It evolved as your--
- That probably helped

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.667
me in some ways.

00:05:55.667 --> 00:05:57.433
But yeah, because
they weren't there.

00:05:57.433 --> 00:05:58.867
And by the way, they were there.

00:05:58.867 --> 00:06:01.533
So a lot of people here,
there's a lot of OGs in here,

00:06:01.533 --> 00:06:04.300
blogging was very
real in 2002, three, four.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.734
I passed on that
because I'm not good at writing

00:06:07.734 --> 00:06:08.734
and if you can't write,

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:10.133
you're not gonna be
successful in blogging.

00:06:10.133 --> 00:06:12.300
And so the thing I
tell everybody in this room

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.734
and people watching at home,

00:06:13.734 --> 00:06:15.834
self-awareness is a
big part of this as well.

00:06:15.834 --> 00:06:16.700
You have to know
what you're good at

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:17.867
and what you're not good at.

00:06:17.867 --> 00:06:20.667
The amount of people
that have started video shows

00:06:20.667 --> 00:06:22.667
'cause they hear
video marketing is good

00:06:22.667 --> 00:06:24.700
but they're scared to
be in front of a camera

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.066
and they're awkward,
that's just not a good strategy.

00:06:27.066 --> 00:06:28.500
It's not your normal place.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:29.667
You have to know yourself.

00:06:29.667 --> 00:06:32.166
And so even though
the mediums were there,

00:06:32.166 --> 00:06:33.700
I could have been a blogger,

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.867
I just knew that i couldn't
communicate very effectively

00:06:35.867 --> 00:06:38.467
in the writing
format and so I punted it,

00:06:38.467 --> 00:06:42.367
kept building a business, and
then when short form Twitter,

00:06:42.367 --> 00:06:44.166
when video for YouTube came out,

00:06:44.166 --> 00:06:45.533
I'm like, that I think I can do,

00:06:45.533 --> 00:06:46.767
and that's what I jumped into.

00:06:46.767 --> 00:06:49.533
- You talk about
hustle all the time, right?

00:06:49.533 --> 00:06:52.667
At what point in the hustle,
if it's just not working,

00:06:52.667 --> 00:06:55.567
do you say maybe I needa
move in another direction?

00:06:55.567 --> 00:06:57.900
- [Gary] That's hard.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.066
When to tell somebody
to quit off of their dream

00:07:01.066 --> 00:07:02.700
or what they wanna
do is very difficult,

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:05.000
'cause the reality is
it coulda been the next day

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.767
that something
good could have happened

00:07:06.767 --> 00:07:07.967
and you just jumped off.

00:07:07.967 --> 00:07:10.400
At the same token,
I equate it to

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.000
American Idol rehearsals

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.033
where people really
think they're great at singing

00:07:15.033 --> 00:07:16.367
and they suck shit.

00:07:16.367 --> 00:07:18.767
I think that there's a
lot of people who think

00:07:18.767 --> 00:07:20.734
if they just keep doing
it they're gonna break out

00:07:20.734 --> 00:07:24.266
and be a micro-celebrity,
and they're not going to

00:07:24.266 --> 00:07:25.600
'cause they
don't have the talent.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.834
I think that's a very
personal answer, Shira.

00:07:27.834 --> 00:07:29.400
I don't think I have the answer

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.133
even if I really know somebody.

00:07:31.133 --> 00:07:32.367
It's a very difficult one,

00:07:32.367 --> 00:07:34.400
that's one that I don't think
I'm in a position to answer.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.600
- First time Gary
does not have an answer.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:39.500
- I don't answer most things.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.700
For people that follow
me, I stay very narrow,

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.367
very deep in my lanes.

00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:45.600
I don't know most things.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:47.800
What I do know is
attention of end consumer.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:49.433
What I do know is
what you're up to,

00:07:49.433 --> 00:07:51.433
what you're not gonna do
and what you're gonna do,

00:07:51.433 --> 00:07:54.000
and that's what I trade on and
that's a good place to play.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.567
- [Shira] So speaking of that,
what are people doing now?

00:07:55.567 --> 00:07:57.467
What are people wanting?

00:07:57.467 --> 00:08:00.734
What does everybody
have to take notice of now?

00:08:00.734 --> 00:08:02.467
Where's Gary playing?

00:08:04.533 --> 00:08:07.400
- This is a mobile-only
world, in my opinion.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:08.834
Here's what I mean by that.

00:08:08.834 --> 00:08:11.834
When I trade attention,
nothing's dead.

00:08:11.834 --> 00:08:14.834
Billboards aren't dead,
radio's not dead,

00:08:14.834 --> 00:08:16.700
television consumption's
not dead.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:18.533
It's the advertising behind them

00:08:18.533 --> 00:08:20.133
that you wanna drive
your business results

00:08:20.133 --> 00:08:21.600
that needs to be debated.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.800
What's overpriced
and what's underpriced?

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:26.333
I think the number
one bet in marketing today

00:08:26.333 --> 00:08:28.000
is buying a
Super Bowl commercial.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.600
I think it is the
number one deal in the world.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:32.066
I think it's
grossly underpriced,

00:08:32.066 --> 00:08:33.000
every person in here

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.467
will know the 30
second story of that brand

00:08:35.467 --> 00:08:38.400
whether they watch it on YouTube
or during the Super Bowl,

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:39.967
and it's an incredible bargain.

00:08:39.967 --> 00:08:42.734
At the same token, I think
the number one worst deal

00:08:42.734 --> 00:08:45.734
in marketing today is
buying programmatic banner

00:08:45.734 --> 00:08:47.700
and pre-roll
ads on the exchanges

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:50.567
and justifying
digital spend on CPM costs.

00:08:50.567 --> 00:08:52.767
Because nobody in
here's paying attention to them

00:08:52.767 --> 00:08:54.867
and we're pouring
ungodly amounts of money to it.

00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:56.300
So that you would
say wait a minute,

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:58.133
Gary's not a digitalist,
he's a traditionalist.

00:08:58.133 --> 00:08:59.900
I'm not, I'm going very narrow.

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:03.967
I think iHeartRadio live
read radio ads

00:09:03.967 --> 00:09:06.133
by the jockey who's
running that station

00:09:06.133 --> 00:09:07.333
is an incredible bargain.

00:09:07.333 --> 00:09:08.633
Radio!

00:09:08.633 --> 00:09:11.233
Meanwhile I think
that back to television,

00:09:11.233 --> 00:09:14.133
I think the Oscars
are the most overpriced ad

00:09:14.133 --> 00:09:18.133
at $2 million a spot because
we want that, as marketers,

00:09:18.133 --> 00:09:20.066
to be the Super Bowl
but it's not.

00:09:20.066 --> 00:09:22.633
The sport of watching
commercials during the Oscars

00:09:22.633 --> 00:09:23.967
is nowhere close to the sport

00:09:23.967 --> 00:09:27.033
of watching commercials
during the Super Bowl.

00:09:27.033 --> 00:09:30.100
So for me, it's mobile only.

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:32.934
Mobile is the only
place where there's true,

00:09:32.934 --> 00:09:36.066
besides Super Bowl,
underpriced attention.

00:09:36.066 --> 00:09:37.734
You've gotta be
very narrow within that.

00:09:37.734 --> 00:09:39.233
I'm obsessed.

00:09:39.233 --> 00:09:41.100
I think everybody
in here will regret

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:44.000
not spending more money on
Facebook ads during this period

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.600
because the average $6 to
$14 CPMs that you see today

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:50.367
are gonna be 50, 60,
$70 in four to five years,

00:09:50.367 --> 00:09:52.100
and everybody's gonna cry.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:55.266
There's people that think
Facebook ads are over right now,

00:09:55.266 --> 00:09:57.266
that are just non-practitioners.

00:09:57.266 --> 00:09:59.000
This is back to my opening line.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:01.367
There are people in
this room that have opinions

00:10:01.367 --> 00:10:05.400
